WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.548
Your bank sends you a text: "Was this you?

2
00:00:05.548 --> 00:00:13.551
300.00 at a shop in Lisbon." Behind it is a program that learned from past payments which ones turned out to be fraud.

3
00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:15.843
Picture the team that built one.

4
00:00:15.843 --> 00:00:20.120
Tested on last month's payments, it caught 98 percent of the fraud.

5
00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:22.865
Then they switched it on, and it caught 62.

6
00:00:23.133 --> 00:00:25.029
Nothing in the code was wrong.

7
00:00:25.029 --> 00:00:26.988
So where did the difference go?

8
00:00:26.988 --> 00:00:28.188
Hold that question.

9
00:00:28.467 --> 00:00:33.478
A model like this learns from labels: past payments, each marked fraud or not.

10
00:00:33.478 --> 00:00:37.653
The mark comes weeks later, when a chargeback arrives or doesn't.

11
00:00:37.867 --> 00:00:44.679
A chargeback is the bank taking the money back from the shop, after the cardholder says, I didn't make this payment.

12
00:00:44.933 --> 00:00:51.724
It also learns from features: numbers about the card, like how many chargebacks it had in the last 30 days.

13
00:00:51.967 --> 00:00:57.442
Every night at two, a job writes one row for the card, counted as of midnight.

14
00:00:57.442 --> 00:00:59.127
For now, the count is 0.

15
00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:04.133
On 10 October at two in the afternoon, someone uses the card for fraud.

16
00:01:04.133 --> 00:01:06.667
Its chargeback arrives on the twelfth.

17
00:01:06.933 --> 00:01:10.076
So from the thirteenth on, the nightly count is 1.

18
00:01:10.333 --> 00:01:12.926
Now build the training row for that fraud.

19
00:01:12.926 --> 00:01:17.246
The easy join takes the card's newest row, the one that's there today.

20
00:01:17.246 --> 00:01:19.591
What number does the training row get?

21
00:01:19.591 --> 00:01:20.579
Pause and guess.

22
00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:23.951
1.

23
00:01:23.951 --> 00:01:27.867
And that 1 is the chargeback this very fraud caused.

24
00:01:28.167 --> 00:01:32.513
So the model learns that fraud comes with a chargeback already on file.

25
00:01:32.513 --> 00:01:36.799
That's like learning to spot a fire by the fire engine parked outside.

26
00:01:37.067 --> 00:01:38.946
It works great on old photos.

27
00:01:38.946 --> 00:01:41.539
It's no help while the fire is starting.

28
00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:43.815
This has a name: label leakage.

29
00:01:43.815 --> 00:01:48.171
Facts from after the decision reach the data the model learns from.

30
00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:51.090
A railway makes the rule easy to see.

31
00:01:51.090 --> 00:01:55.816
A passenger says she missed her connection at 14:00 last Tuesday.

32
00:01:56.100 --> 00:02:03.335
To judge whether she picked a sensible train, you'd look at the timetable that was on the board that Tuesday, not today's.

33
00:02:03.335 --> 00:02:06.716
Today's board knows about changes she couldn't have seen.

34
00:02:06.933 --> 00:02:07.396
Map it.

35
00:02:07.396 --> 00:02:10.896
The passenger's Tuesday is the moment of the payment.

36
00:02:10.896 --> 00:02:13.670
Each night's row is the board on that day.

37
00:02:13.670 --> 00:02:16.048
And the newest row is today's board.

38
00:02:16.267 --> 00:02:18.576
So, look back from each label instead.

39
00:02:18.576 --> 00:02:22.467
Take the newest row that was there at the moment of the payment.

40
00:02:22.733 --> 00:02:25.430
For this fraud, that's the 10 October row: 0.

41
00:02:25.430 --> 00:02:28.426
That's what the model would have read at the till.

42
00:02:28.426 --> 00:02:30.583
This is called a point in time join.

43
00:02:30.833 --> 00:02:31.812
So far so good.

44
00:02:31.812 --> 00:02:33.836
But each row carries two times.

45
00:02:33.836 --> 00:02:36.382
One is the moment it's about: midnight.

46
00:02:36.382 --> 00:02:39.841
The other is when it was written: two in the morning.

47
00:02:40.067 --> 00:02:43.507
Take a payment at one in the morning on 13 October.

48
00:02:43.507 --> 00:02:47.217
The 13 October row is about midnight, which is earlier.

49
00:02:47.217 --> 00:02:49.376
But it wasn't written until two.

50
00:02:49.376 --> 00:02:50.995
At one, it didn't exist.

51
00:02:51.233 --> 00:02:55.336
So the join skips it, and takes the 12 October row: 0.

52
00:02:55.600 --> 00:02:56.705
It gets worse.

53
00:02:56.705 --> 00:03:04.360
On 15 October, a late chargeback report comes in, and the team works out 12 October again: now 1.

54
00:03:04.633 --> 00:03:08.805
The correction is about the twelfth, but it was written on the fifteenth.

55
00:03:08.805 --> 00:03:10.919
Our one o'clock payment still gets 0.

56
00:03:11.133 --> 00:03:14.977
It's a notice pinned to the board on Friday, dated Tuesday.

57
00:03:14.977 --> 00:03:16.736
The passenger never saw it.

58
00:03:17.033 --> 00:03:18.804
So the rule has three parts.

59
00:03:18.804 --> 00:03:22.913
Take the newest row that's about a time at or before the payment.

60
00:03:23.167 --> 00:03:26.011
And that was written at or before the payment, too.

61
00:03:26.300 --> 00:03:31.576
And ignore rows older than a time limit, after which a value counts as missing.

62
00:03:31.867 --> 00:03:42.327
The lesson's demo runs this on a month of payments: 150,000 of them, on 5,000 cards, 2,378 of them fraud.

63
00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:46.873
With the newest row, every single payment got a value written after it.

64
00:03:47.133 --> 00:03:53.924
The rule, flag the payment if the card has had a chargeback, catches 98 percent of the fraud on that data.

65
00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:57.550
On the point in time data, it catches 62.

66
00:03:57.550 --> 00:03:59.675
And live, at the till, 62.

67
00:03:59.933 --> 00:04:01.864
That's where the difference went.

68
00:04:01.864 --> 00:04:02.976
It was never there.

69
00:04:03.467 --> 00:04:05.827
You rarely write this join by hand.

70
00:04:05.827 --> 00:04:14.595
Feature stores, programs that keep features for models, like Feast, do it for you, and DuckDB, a small database, has an ASOF JOIN.

71
00:04:14.867 --> 00:04:16.811
But check what time they look at.

72
00:04:16.811 --> 00:04:19.816
By default, Feast looks at the time a row is about.

73
00:04:19.816 --> 00:04:24.529
Keeping out values written later, like our correction, is a setting you turn on.

74
00:04:24.767 --> 00:04:28.595
So: train only on what the model could have read at that moment.

75
00:04:28.595 --> 00:04:30.808
Anything written later doesn't count.

76
00:04:30.808 --> 00:04:33.021
No fire engines in the training data.

77
00:04:33.467 --> 00:04:34.583
One quick check.

78
00:04:34.583 --> 00:04:38.492
A payment at half past one in the morning on 14 October.

79
00:04:38.492 --> 00:04:41.145
Which row does it get, and what value?

80
00:04:41.145 --> 00:04:42.261
Pause and think.

81
00:04:45.500 --> 00:04:47.236
The 13 October row: 1.

82
00:04:47.236 --> 00:04:52.681
The 14 October row is about midnight, but it isn't written until two.
